stellar-wind-sonic-point-ident-ade06cfb.symmetry_origin:
The Sonic Point Radius arises from the principle of mass conservation applied to a control volume around the nozzle throat. For a steady, one-dimensional, isentropic flow, the mass flow rate (\r
) is constant throughout the nozzle. At the sonic throat (where \r = \r_s
), the flow velocity is equal to the speed of sound (\ri = \ri_s
). The sonic condition (\ri = \ri_s
) is a consequence of maximizing the mass flow rate for a given stagnation condition, which occurs at the point where the derivative of the velocity with respect to the area is infinite in the nozzle flow equations. This is mathematically found by considering the energy and continuity equations and differentiating to find the conditions for maximum mass flow.

stellar-wind-sonic-point-ident-ade06cfb.limits_and_boundary:
As the mass flow rate (\r
) approaches zero, the sonic point radius (\r_s
) would also approach zero, implying no flow or a vanishing throat size for any measurable flow. Conversely, a very high mass flow rate would necessitate a larger sonic point radius to accommodate the flow at sonic conditions. The concept is most relevant for choked flow conditions, where the mass flow rate is limited by the sonic conditions at the throat.
